    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Good morning from WKYT News. I'm Bill Bryant and we welcome you to Kentucky Newsmakers and hope you're enjoying your weekend. Our guest today is Congressman Andy Barr, who represents Central Kentucky in Washington. He was re-elected in November. He's a Republican. 2021 has already been eventful in our nation's capital. starting with the attack on the seat of government on January 6. Legislation has also been passed to deal with the economic fallout of the pandemic, at least in the House, and that includes $1,400 stimulus checks for Americans. But that was passed without a single Republican vote. We'll talk with Congressman Barr about what he thinks a relief bill should have looked like. A minimum wage hike proposal apparently is still in the works. Barr also recently introduced the Carol Act. That is short for cardiovascular advances in research and opportunity's legacy. It is named after bars late wife who died of a heart ailment at the age of 39 last year. And we're coming off a very rough stretch of weather here in Kentucky from ice storms to flooding and there is a request going to Washington for two federal disaster declarations. Congressman Barr is joining us now. Welcome, Congressman. Thank you very much for coming. Good to be with you. Let's start with that because of the flooding and these two terrible ice storms that roll through another winter storm. There's all kinds of damage in Kentucky. Are you supportive of what apparently will be two requests for federal disaster aid from Washington that requires the signature of the president? Absolutely, and first of all, our thoughts and prayers go to all of our constituents who have been impacted by these historic floods. Many counties in the Sixth Congressional District in particular, Esto County and Powell County in the eastern part of the Sixth District have been significantly impacted. We've been in regular communication, both me personally and my staff with the local officials there and property owners. We are encouraging all of those individuals to document the damage, the flooding that's gone on there. I'm going to be heading down to Estel County and Powell County later this afternoon with some bottled water and some supplies, cleaning supplies to help pitch in. But the main focus right now is to document the damage, document the flooding, get that to the local emergency management officials, and then support that with an application coming from Frankfort to President Biden to seek an emergency declaration so that FEMA can come in and provide emergency assistance. The federal delegation is in full support of that request and we will be joining a letter with my other colleagues in the U.S. House. in support of the governor's request of President Biden to declare this emergency so that we can get emergency assistance to our constituents as soon as possible. Your message is that it's important that Kentucky and show proof and that if there is damage to property for businesses or individuals that they need to get that documented and get it to their local emergency folks quickly. That's right. And there is a portal in these counties, a local emergency management, county emergency management portals where individuals can submit that documentation that supports the county level declaration of an emergency. And that will be funneled up to Frankfurt for a comprehensive request. to the federal government for assistance. We will be encouraging folks to do that on our social media and website. But for anyone from those counties who has suffered damage, whether it's a business or a home, we encourage you to document that and send that into your local emergency management portal so that we can get you the assistance that you deserve. Do you assume that can be turned around quickly that the president is likely to sign it fast and that that aid can start coming to Kentucky fairly fast? I do anticipate that we will get it because the damage is so extensive and that we will get it quickly. However, the quicker we can get the evidence and documentation into the county emergency management, folks, and to Frankfurt, the quicker the governor can get the request into the federal government. All right. And then we will be supporting that, of course, at the federal level. Alright, so a lot of folks with some quick work to do in response to this, which has really been a tough time. Congressman, you have two young daughters. It's been a tough year for your family, I know, several months now after they lost their mother and your wife's very unexpected death. You are proposing something you're calling the Carol Act. right now that is in her memory, but it is designed to do some concrete things. What would that be? Well, Carol leaves an amazing legacy as a wonderful wife, mother, and friend, but we want to carry forward her amazing legacy. Her life was cut too short, but it was a life of incredible impact and consequence. And we know that that legacy is going to continue going forward. And the CAROL Act, the Cardiovascular Advances in Research and Opportunity Legacy Act, which I introduced is going to carry for that legacy and make a difference and hopefully save lives of other families touched by heart disease. We knew that Carol was diagnosed with underlying heart condition, a heart valve disease since she was in seventh grade, but we, like many other families, had no idea that this was a life-threatening situation, and that's the problem. That's why we need more medical research. The Carol Act would provide a specific line of effort, a grant program within the National Heart Lung Blood Institute. We've collaborated with the NHLBI and Dr. Gary Gibbons there, the federal agency that provides funding for research for heart disease. And we know we need specific precision medicine and investigation into how to detect a life-threatening situation and differentiate that from mitral valve prolapse or other heart valve diseases that are typically benign. And in Carol's case, her underlying condition, mitralval prolapse, it only results in sudden cardiac death in 0.2 percent of the cases. It mostly impacts young women. We don't know why. And so that's why we need more research. And this legislation would authorize $100 million over five years to help investigators, help cardiologists, help physicians identify those risk factors that would put an individual like my wife in a life-threatening situation. And we're very happy that in just one week's time, we have over 75 bipartisan co-sponsors. My colleague, Congresswoman Kathleen Rice, a Democrat from New York has generously agreed to help lead this charge with me. And again, this is our way of honoring the extraordinary legacy of my wife, but we want to do some real good in helping other families avoid a similar tragedy. I know Carol, I had a great career in her own right. She was in medical sales. If she had had the kind of information that you think can come out of an act like this in Congress, would you been better prepared to deal with the situation that she faced? There's no question. And that's the goal of the legislation is to give patients like Carol better information, better tools for intervention, surgical procedures, perhaps or a treatment plan that can allow them to leave long healthy lives. And in fact, Many Americans who do have this condition with interventions, with a treatment program, can in fact survive a hard bowel disease. And that's the message that we want to impart on our constituents and other Americans around the country. Go get checked. Early prevention is important. making sure that we can take this condition very, very seriously. It's typically not life threatening, but we want everyone to take it very, very seriously so that you can get the treatment that you need. Many want to know, but don't want to really pry into your private family matters. Are your girls doing okay now? Well, thanks so much for the question, because they are. They're amazingly resilient, and I got home from DC last night, got a chance to take them to school. Eleanor is ready for her social studies debate in class today, and Mary Clay is, I think, ready for her spelling test. And so they missed their mommy terribly, as do I, of course. But they are doing what their mother would want them to do. her greatest legacy and they're tough like their mama and we are charging forward and we miss her but we know we can honor her legacy by just getting it done. That's what she always said to us, just get it done and that's exactly what we're doing. Congressman can be said that there are a lot of avenues, you know, where we could be doing more research. President Biden has said he wants to make a major push on cancer research, and some in science say that, you know, look at the rapid development. We had these COVID vaccines that if we applied ourselves, we could do more. Do you support efforts to incentivize companies to do more research? Absolutely, and I think Operation Warp Speed over the last 12 months is a great example that when there is a robust public-private partnership that amazing things can happen. Think about this. In just 12 months' time, we've deployed three effective and safe vaccines that are literally ending this pandemic in probably six months' time. We know we have more work to do, but it is a testament to investment in medical research and medical innovation. It is not just the public sector, it's part of the private sector as well, but a partnership there. And so I have always supported investments in the National Institutes of Health, and now with my own life of being personally touched by this, I have recommitted and redoubled my efforts to support medical research and innovation at places like the University of Kentucky. We have the Marquee Cancer Center, we have the Sanders Brown Center on Aging, And of course now with heart disease impacting my life, I am so committed to the mission of the National Heart Lung Blood Institute. Let's talk about where we are as a country right now. The events of January 6, the shock to the nation and have resulted in arrests and pending prosecutions. You were at the Capitol that day. How has it come to this in this country? Well of course what a tragic event on January 6th and of course the violence that we saw in the cities around the country last summer related to the Black Lives Matter movement also very very tragic and what we need to remember in this country is that We resolve our differences at the ballot box. We resolve our differences in courtrooms. We resolve our differences peacefully through debate and the First Amendment. And censorship is not the answer. We need to restore confidence in our elections. That's the way through all of this. And violence is never the answer. And we obviously need to hold all of those individuals who resort to violence to full account. But it is tragic that our country has become so polarized and divided, and I have many friends on the other side of the aisle, and we're talking to one another about how it is that we can bring the country back together. And I'm excited because, as the co-chair of the bipartisan Working Group and Congress, my friend and colleague from Washington State, Democrat Derek Kilmer and I are reconvening our Working Group next Tuesday morning. We do this every week. We get together for breakfast as a bipartisan group, and we talk. about opportunities for overlapping interest and bringing the country together. I think that dialogue is a good thing. That's what we need to do. But I will tell you, it doesn't help when we have partisan legislation in Washington. It doesn't help. when we have legislation that will actually undermine confidence in our elections and frankly it doesn't help to have a wall a giant wall surrounding the u.s. capital building when there's no there's no real threat anymore and the intelligence doesn't support any major threat i don't like the idea of a of a wall surrounding the people's house in washington d.c. when there is no imminent threat to the building that that threat seems to have passed i don't like the idea of politicians hypocritically supporting a wall to protect themselves, but not supporting a wall to protect the American people at the southern border. It's my understanding that the Capitol Police have requested that National Guard troops remain there for a couple of more months. You are not supportive of that? Well, look, I certainly defer to the Capitol Hill Police and what they need. We needed, frankly, better security posture on January 6th. And it was an intelligence failure. And we need to listen to our Capitol Hill Police and security to make sure that we do secure the people and the building there, of course. But the long-term solution is not a wall around the Capitol building. We need to revisit some of the security measures that we have there. But eventually we want to return to an open capital that the American people can visit and do so in a safe way. Alright, Congressman Andy Bars with us a lot more questions to come. We'll talk about the COVID relief package passed solely by Democrats. No Republican support for that. We'll be back on WKYT's Kentucky Newsmakers in a moment. Welcome back to WKYT's Kentucky Newsmakers. We're certainly glad you're with us today. And Congressman Andy Barr, Republican, who represents Central Kentucky is our guest today and will continue our discussion. So Congressman, the COVID relief package was passed solely by Democrats. No Republican support. It included those $1,400 stimulus checks. It's also nearly $2 trillion when you look at the bill in total. You voted against it. Were there parts of it that you could have supported? There is a lot of money in there coming to states like Kentucky for state and local governments, for instance. Well, I certainly would have supported some of the targeted measures related to vaccine distribution. That's really what we need to focus on. But what's so disappointing about this partisan, hyper-partisan bill that will add well over two trillion, with interest, well over two trillion dollars to the national debt is that the vast majority of the bill was completely unrelated to COVID. It's said that only 9% of this legislation went to vaccine distribution and to the health care response to the pandemic. You know, I was proud to support five separate bipartisan bills from the CARES Act forward to that $900 billion relief package that we passed just before the end of the year. That total $4 trillion And what's so disappointing, Bill, about this partisan effort now is that over $1 trillion, think about that, $1 trillion of the previous bipartisan efforts remains unspent. So I think we need to focus on actually deploying the resources that we've already appropriated. And if we do need more of funding for vaccine distribution, we need to have a targeted response to help do that. But remember, thanks to the previous administration and Operation Warp Speed, we have today, before any bill has been passed under the Biden administration, we have one and a half million shots of vaccines going into arms every single day in this country. And thanks to the bipartisan efforts of the last Congress, we have now deployed over $600 billion in emergency, forgivable loans to small businesses. So much has already been done. And we're predicted to be back at full economic strength by the middle of 2021. So this looks a bit like a profligate spending. And again, most of the bill is not related to COVID at all. But one of the things that I'm really concerned about is over $350 billion of this legislation goes to bail out mismanaged blue states and cities for pre-COVID liabilities. Well that comes the question, could you have supported the aid to state and local governments who in some cases have incurred tremendous expense during this pandemic, but you're making the point that in some cases states and local governments already had the liabilities. Oh, they had liabilities well preceding COVID-19 and it's not fair to states that actually managed their budgets well before COVID-19 to now bail them out. But here's the point, Bill. You know, in a bipartisan way through the CARES Act and the four other bipartisan bills, we deployed trillions of dollars, trillions of dollars to state local governments, not just in the form of direct aid, but also in the form of money from the housing and urban development, from the Department of Health and Human Services, a direct funding for hospitals. And so, look, I'm open to more assistance to state local governments if it's related to the pandemic. But if it's related to just bailing them out for pension liabilities that they mismanaged before the pandemic, I'm not really interested in that. And we also created a municipal liquidity facility that was underutilized because, frankly, many cities and states were actually doing quite well with their revenues. So the bottom line is, I support, and we're not out of the woods yet, we continue to respond to this pandemic, but there is a trillion dollars of unspent money And if any of my cities or counties or hospitals need assistance, we have a lot of money that has not yet been spent. We will help them apply for that money and help get them anything that they need. But this is piling on a huge burden on future taxpayers, $2 trillion. And with interest, another $1.3 trillion over 10 years, this will weaken our country over the long run. President Biden clearly is making a push on climate change. He has rejoined the Paris Climate Accord, the administration is calling for more use of renewable energy going forward. Do you have fears about how that could impact Kentucky, which has been such a provider of coal over the years? Well, we saw what happened in Texas, where a lack of grid reliability resulted in a terrible situation for the citizens down there. And I think it's a warning that while renewable energy certainly should be part of our energy mix and our portfolio, and while no one is suggesting that climate is not a legitimate issue to talk about, we need to have common sense about this. And if we completely abandon fossil energy, we're going to see blackouts and brownouts like we saw in Texas. And so I do worry that the extreme climate activists have hijacked this administration. And on the Financial Services Committee, I'm particularly interested in the way in which this administration is going to use financial regulation to choke off financing for legitimate law-abiding businesses. you know i don't think that the radical green new deal proposal can pass even a majority of the house in the senate majority democrat house and senate because it's so extreme it's so radical And so what we're looking at is an administration that set up a climate czar in the Department of Treasury emphasis on climate disclosures and securities filings. And so what we think they're going to do and what they're basically predicting ahead or telegraphing for us is that they're going to use the backdoor of financial regulation to pressure banks And other financial firms to deprive energy companies, manufacturing companies, yes coal and fossil energy companies of access to capital. And I think politicizing access to capital is very dangerous. It's an extension of cancel culture, whether in big tech. or in the academy and universities or in the mainstream media, we're seeing that extended into banking and in finance in a way that I think is very dangerous, discriminating against law-abiding American companies in order to bankrupt them. It's not about predicting financial stress, which is what the Biden administration is saying. It's about creating financial stress for the American people. A couple more issues I want to get to here. You know, we watch a lot of hot spots around the world right now, the Middle East, obviously. The rivalries with China and Russia. The concerns, as you mentioned earlier, along the U.S. Southern border. What are you keeping your eyes on right now? Well, as a new member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee and as a member of the House China Task Force, I think the rise of China is the greatest threat. The pandemic was a huge wake-up call for the United States. This was a plague that was unleashed on the world by the Chinese Communist Party. We've seen what they've done in terms of ripping up an international treaty with Hong Kong, pushing an expansionist, aggressive expansionist, posture in the lesser developed part of the world. There are human rights violations and genocide against religious minorities like the Uighurs, and the growth of their hostile, aggressive growth in their military, their theft of intellectual property. We have learned from the pandemic that we need to strengthen the resiliency of our supply chain. China, the Chinese Communist Party, weaponized the supply chain against us to make the pandemic even worse. We need to ensure a lot of our manufacturing. So, I think that's number one. And I'll tell you, it's just so alarming that Washington, D.C., is so partisan now, I offered a simple amendment to the $1.9 trillion stimulus bill because there's billions of dollars in there for 4 and 8. I simply said, I don't want any of that funding to go to the Chinese Communist Party, and on a unanimous party-line vote, all Democrats voted against prohibiting any of the foreign aid from going to an entity or individual supported by the Chinese Communist Party. That is alarming to me because we need to be united as a country to confront the real threat from a rising China, a China that's threatening us militarily from an economic standpoint, certainly from technology and the theft of intellectual property from a trade standpoint. So we need to get serious about this issue. And I'm going to focus my time and energy on the Asia Pacific subcommittee, countering the rising China threat. I don't think you'll get any argument from anybody anywhere that Washington is too partisan, certainly right now on all sides. All right. Kentucky Secretary of State Michael Adams says the next year's elections will be running new districts. He says the lines can and will be drawn before the filing deadline. The sixth has grown. could have to shed some voters. Will it be tough for you if those lines shift around some and we have just a few seconds on that? I'm less worried about that bill and more interested in making sure that we don't lose a congressional district. It's important for Kentucky to preserve the six that we have. We're told that based on the preliminary numbers that we will be keeping all six members of the house. That's a good thing. Obviously the legislature will decide those lines and we have a privilege of representing these 19 counties. We love serving the people of these 19 counties if we have to lose because of the census data. That's regrettable, but we obviously have to honor the one person, one vote principle. And there is a constitutional requirement about that. So we'll be certainly engaged with the General Assembly and with our colleagues to make sure that we continue to get the opportunity to represent as much of Central and Eastern Kentucky as possible. Congressman Andy Barr is always thanks for being with us. We appreciate it. And we'll be back on Kentucky Newsmakers. Welcome back to Kentucky Newsmakers a year ago this weekend, the first case of COVID-19 showed up in Kentucky. After a huge effort to quickly develop effective vaccines, producers are now ahead of schedule. Pharmaceutical giants, even some fierce rivals, are joining forces to help Americans. Our Chief National Political Analyst, Greta Van Suster, explains. Hello, I'm Greta Vance Sustron, and here is your full-court fast break. The race to vaccinate is speeding up. The nation's first single-dose COVID shot made by Johnson & Johnson is going into American arms. Tuesday, President Biden announcing that by the end of May, there will be enough vaccines for every adult in America. The administration advanced the timeline by roughly two months, pushing Johnson & Johnson into around-the-clock production, offering logistical help from the Department of Defense. The goal? To produce 94 million Johnson-Johnson single-dose shots by the end of May. That's on top of Pfizer and Moderna's doses. Together, those two manufacturers are pledging to provide by the end of May enough shots for 200 million Americans. But it's important to remember that while the doses may be ready by the end of May, it could take longer to distribute and administer. President Biden also announcing more important progress for the second half of the year. The world's second largest vaccine manufacturer, Merck, is teaming up with rival Johnson & Johnson to boost vaccine production. Merck is dedicating two of its facilities to Johnson & Johnson's vaccine. It will likely take months for everything to get up and running, but Merck could help create booster shots to tackle the new and more contagious rapidly spreading COVID variants. It could also produce doses for teens and children. Assuming Johnson and Johnson's vaccine is deemed safe for younger age groups. Want more full court press? Tune in Sunday. We bring politics home covering the national stories that impact you. And that's Kentucky Newsmakers. Thank you so much for joining us. We'll see you bright and early this week on the morning news. Have a good week ahead.",
